A||||6||Ironhorse||Do not stone me to death if I messed up||||1||
Z||000000||Ironhorse||08-10-2000||11:04 PM||||Hello to you all fieroholic.  I got a couple of things goin on my v6.  I found the problem with vaccume hoses, map sensor, and mat sensor which is causing my problem.  I got 2 question to ask now and I sincerely hope that I don't get flammed for this.  I have read in 3 different service book for Fiero.  When I was setting my lifter rods.  The book said to keep spinning the lifter rod till you can't turn it anymore then turn the bolt 1 and half turn.  Everybody I talked to said that I'm a nutcase for doing something like this.  Am I wrong?  Second question.. When I drive my fiero at 30 mph, my rpm run at 3000 rpm.  The tranny is full to the proper line on dipstick, and I installed the shift kit on this automatic transmission.  What did I do worng here?    Thanks for any of your help||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||08-11-2000||12:25 AM||||It sounds like you did it right.<P>Generally, you try and turn the push rod with two fingers while tightening the rocker. Obviously you can really crank down on the rocker and still spin the rod, but what you want is to just take out the slop. <P>Lightly turn the rod and tighten the rocker until the rod just stops turning without really trying to turn it to hard, then add 1.5 turns to the rocker. Various people will argue 1.5 is too much and there are good arguments to 3/4 to 1.5 turns, but the manual spec is good enough to 90% of the situations. <P>Unless I mis-read or missed something, you should be fine.<P>On the trans, it sounds like it's not shifting. Is the TVS cable adjusted correctly? Will it upshift if you let up on the throttle?<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ironhorse||08-11-2000||12:45 AM||||Terryk,  On that trans, It feels like it's not shifting. When I let up on the gas, the car slow down but I do not feel the upshift when I let up.  Also I forgot to mention that I put in new torque convertor lock up in as well as shift kit.    When I put it in gear, I lose about 500 rpm at idle, when I shift it in N or P, I gain back that 500 rpm... A lot of time when I put it in gear, after about 3 or 4 sec of idle in drive, it dies||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||08-11-2000||08:00 AM||||Just a hunch but try disconnecting the lock-up soleniod and take it for a ride||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||08-11-2000||08:17 AM||||With the rockers- I adjust mine pretty much the same way, only I don't go by turning them, since one guy may be able to apply alot more torque with his fingers than another, thus requiring the rocker be tightened more to complete that first step. I don't think adjustments should be based on the strength of the mechanic!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Plus, it would take more to jam up a rod on a new lifter, that hasn't been pumped up, than an old one that has, and is completely full of oil.<BR>I adjust mine by moving them up and down, not roatating them. As soon as I feel there no verticle movement, then another turn and a half to center the lifter. <BR>Bottom line is, for the most part, if it's quiet, they're probably OK.<P>I don't know what kind of trans kit you installed, but, maybe you got something in backwards, sounds like you aren't getting fluid flow somewhere. Might need to pull the valve body off and verify all your springs and check valves are oriented properly.<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ironhorse||08-11-2000||09:22 PM||||*sigh* I did everything right on the vaccum lines and it still goes crazy.  I found out that I have bad MAT and MAP sensor. I gotta shell out some cash to get oil pressure sensor, TPS, MAP, MAT, IAC.  Those are the only thing that I did not replace while I was rebuilding the engine.  I was tryin to save money.. I guess it didn't work. Then I'm gonna go from there||||reg||9
